WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:03.429 align:start position:0%
 
in 2004  historic wildfires created a cloak of  smoke  over alaskan forests as the climate has  warmed  such fires have gotten worse in alaska  where spruce trees have fed hungry  flames for thousands of years  large blazes nearly doubled on average  from the 1960s  until 2015. these fires unlock the  soil's carbon stores  and release them into the atmosphere  theoretically  that carbon can lead to long-term  warming and even more fires  yet new research offers a glimmer of  hope it suggests that when spruce trees  die  the trees that replace them leafy birch  and aspen  may curb wildfires and slow carbon  leakage from their habitat  the 2004 fires scorched seven times the  50-year average land area  for alaskan burns this created a vast  natural laboratory for ecologists who  study forests  they knew that deciduous trees such as  birch and aspen  tended to sprout up in the embers of  burned-out coniferous spruces  but they needed to know more about this  transition to understand how the climate  and ecosystem might change  over a long time say a century  from 2005 to 2017  researchers scoured 75 sites where  spruce dominated before the 2004  fires they estimated the total amount of  seedlings from species of spruce  birch and aspen to determine how the  sites transitioned  by 2017 only 28  of the sites returned to spruce  dominance 72  became either a mix of spruce and  deciduous  or deciduous dominated the researchers  measured tree diameters  to estimate the amount of carbon and  nitrogen stored above ground  they also estimated the amount of carbon  and nitrogen trapped in the soil  to do this they compared the depths of  burned  formerly carbon-rich soil layers to  those of new layers  and measured the heights of roots  disturbed by the fires  but they still needed more data to  forecast forest turnover  the researchers panned detailed alaskan  forest fire records  dating to the 1940s and identified burn  sites once dominated by spruce  now in different stages of transition  they traveled to the sites to collect  data  then combine them with data from the  2004 burn sites  the combined data helped them estimate  forest trajectories  over a hundred years this showed forests  that transitioned to birch  and aspen would accumulate carbon four  times faster than forests dominated by  black spruce  most carbon would be in the trees  themselves since deciduous trees gobble  up soil nutrients  as they grow rapidly spruce trees grow  slow and steady leaving a wealth of  organic soil  that readily ignites the live fast die  young approach of aspen and birch  leaves less fuel for wildfires to chew  up and spit out into the atmosphere  as carbon dioxide deciduous trees are  set to dominate the interior alaskan  forests  by the end of this century still unknown  is whether birch and aspen forest will  stave off wildfires  as the region becomes warmer since just  about anything can burn  in very dry hot weather if this forest  turnover  does lead to a less fiery alaskan  landscape  carbon may continue to pool amid the  shady canopy  of the great north's future forests  you

